WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.950
AI tools for scouting and player evaluation. D2.4 Implementation Guide · Video 2 of
4 · Chapter 3.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:16.641
What an AI scouting tool does. It learns patterns from past data, for example which
players an academy selected in earlier seasons, and applies them to new players. Its

3
00:00:16.691 --> 00:00:19.950
output is usually a score, a ranking or a shortlist.

4
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:28.798
Two limits matter most. Even a well-built model predicts only moderately well: the
best model in a German academy reached an AUC of 0.69 (0.5 = chance, 1.0 = perfect).

5
00:00:28.848 --> 00:00:35.950
Most published models have not been tested on outside data. A tool trained on past
selections can learn the biases in those selections.

6
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:44.950
Small-academy option. No AI tool? Use a structured scouting template: the same five
to eight criteria, scored the same way, for every player.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:54.032
Before you start. A written problem statement and a named data owner. Player
records: code, date of birth, position, assessments. A data protection check

8
00:00:54.082 --> 00:00:57.950
(scoring minors may need a DPIA). Access to the tool and a laptop.

9
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:00.950
Once, before the first season.

10
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:10.774
Step 1. Ask the supplier three questions in writing. How was the model validated? On
how many players? On data from another club or season? Check: You have the answers

11
00:01:10.824 --> 00:01:11.950
on file, in writing.

12
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:20.950
Step 2. Check what the tool actually does. Face recognition, emotion recognition or
other biometric functions bring stricter rules. Ask your DPO before you buy.

13
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:28.950
Step 3. Give staff a short AI-literacy briefing (about 30 minutes). Cover what the
score is, what it is not, and who decides.

14
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:38.933
Step 4. Set up player records with a code, date of birth and, where you can, a
maturity estimate. Without them you cannot check the tool for relative age and

15
00:01:38.983 --> 00:01:39.950
maturation bias.

16
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:47.950
Step 5. Write a one-line definition for every data field. A GPS sprint speed and a
stopwatch sprint speed are different numbers.

17
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:54.950
Step 6. Record the tool version and the date in your log. When the supplier updates
the model, scores can shift.

18
00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:57.950
Every scouting cycle.

19
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:03.950
Step 7. Enter or import the new assessments. Soon after each observation, the same
way every time.

20
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:12.950
Step 8. Check data completeness before you look at any score. A high score on a
half-empty record is not comparable with one on a complete record.

21
00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:22.424
Step 9. Generate the shortlist and send it to a named scout. The tool only screens.
A person reviews every flagged case and takes the decision. Check: Each player on

22
00:02:22.474 --> 00:02:24.950
the shortlist has a recorded human decision.

23
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:33.950
Step 10. Once a season, compare the shortlist by birth quarter and maturity status.
If early-born or early-maturing players dominate, the tool may be reproducing bias.

24
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:37.450
Figure 3.1  A shortlist screen. 1  The score is one input, not a decision.

25
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:40.950
Figure 3.1  A shortlist screen. 2  Low data completeness lowers trust in the score.

26
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:44.450
Figure 3.1  A shortlist screen. 3  Check scores by birth quarter.

27
00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:47.950
Figure 3.1  A shortlist screen. 4  A named scout records the human decision.

28
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:58.628
Interpreting AI outputs (1/2). A score is one input. The data narrows the choice and
people decide. Missing data is a hidden bias. Check whose data is absent. Check for

29
00:02:58.678 --> 00:03:02.950
the relative age effect: early-born players are more often selected.

30
00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:12.523
Interpreting AI outputs (2/2). Maturation is a separate bias. Check outputs by birth
quarter and by maturity status. Ask about calibration. If the supplier cannot

31
00:03:12.573 --> 00:03:15.950
explain it, treat the number as a rank, not a probability.

32
00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:26.187
Watch out: automated decisions about children. Do not let the tool decide on its own
whether a young player is selected or released. A qualified person reviews the

33
00:03:26.237 --> 00:03:28.950
evidence, takes the decision and records it.

34
00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:39.769
In the weekly routine. Let the tool narrow 60 names to 15, then watch those 15 live.
One weekly shortlist review (about 20 minutes) on MD-2. One line per player to

35
00:03:39.819 --> 00:03:43.950
coaches: "Flagged by the tool; watched twice; invite to trial."

36
00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:51.110
Common problems. Shortlist is almost all early-born players: Run the birth-quarter
check (step 10); review late-born players manually. Scores changed for everyone

37
00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:58.491
overnight: Check your log (step 6); do not compare across the update. A player with
little data has a high score: Check completeness (step 8); do not act on incomplete

38
00:03:58.541 --> 00:03:59.950
records. All problems: Table 3.1.

39
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:09.198
Quick start by role. Coach: Never cut or promote a player on a score alone. Scout:
Record a decision for every flagged player, every week. Performance staff: Supply

40
00:04:09.248 --> 00:04:14.950
birth dates and maturity estimates. Academy manager: Get the supplier's validation
answers in writing.

